---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

export const title = "Action is the Antidote";
export const date = "2026-08-28";
export const excerpt =
  "I was feeling AI anxiety. The fix wasn't more reading — it was building something, anything, to see what it actually felt like.";
export const tags = ["ai", "building", "learning", "2026"];
---

<SiteLayout title={`${title} — Saachi K Gupta`}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p style="opacity:0.7;">{date}</p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    For a while I was experiencing a specific kind of AI anxiety — not fear of the technology, but overwhelm at the pace of it. Every week a new model, a new workflow, a new thing everyone said you needed to learn. The more I read, the further behind I felt.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    At some point I stopped reading about it and started building with it. The point was never to become an engineer. It was to understand the hype from the inside, by doing something real with it instead of just observing from the outside. Action turned out to be the only thing that actually helped.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Things I've wanted to build</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Once I started thinking this way, the ideas came fast. Some of them are half-formed, some are actual projects:
  </p>

  <ul style="max-width: 75ch;">
    <li>An interactive permit map — what permits exist for what parks, trails, and wilderness areas, and a calendar for when applications open, so you're not scrambling the week the lottery drops</li>
    <li>A seating chart optimizer — using research on weak ties and network theory to surface introductions, not just seat people who already know each other next to each other</li>
  </ul>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    [More on what you've actually built — link to builds section, the interview coach skill, jobs dashboard, etc.]
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">What 2026 is about</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    2026 is a year of taking steps — no matter how small. I've been thinking about why we stop trying new things as adults. When you're a kid, the whole structure of your life is built around being exposed to new activities in case one of them catches. At some point that stops, and you have to manufacture the same thing deliberately.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Vision boarding helps with this more than I expected. Saying what you want out loud — or writing it down somewhere people can see it — gives other people the opportunity to be helpful. It also creates a kind of accountability that feels more generative than pressuring. This year I want to bike the North Cascades Highway. I'm writing it here so I have to mean it.
  </p>

</SiteLayout>
